    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d53">

                <pb facs="0166" n="104" sameAs="frus:frus1945v01/pg104"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.CC/3–345: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-03-03T13:00:00+01:00">March 3, 1945—1
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 5:25 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1004. For Assistant Secretary Dunn.
                    Referring to our conversation last evening Bidault instructed Catroux<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d53-f1"/> to endeavor to persuade the Russians to change their point
                    of view, but he is not very optimistic as to the outcome.</p>
                <p>
                    Bidault himself is on the spot here
                    because he obtained the approval of de Gaulle and the
                    Council of Ministers for the Cadogan
                        formula<anchor n="4" xml:id="d53-f2"/> under an impression he said he had
                    received at London that it was acceptable to the Russians, although I warned him
                    that the Russians had to be consulted first. There was opposition in the Council
                    of Ministers to approving that formula but Bidault won out with de Gaulle’s support.
                    Now de Gaulle will not agree to his going back to the
                    Council of Ministers and telling them the formula they had approved had not been
                    cleared with the Russians. Bidault is in a
                    bad jam and does not know what to do about it.</p>
                <p>I explained the situation late last night to
                        Bogomolov,<anchor n="5" xml:id="d53-f3"/> who came to
                    see me, but he has nothing on the subject from Moscow; nor has Duff
                        Cooper been kept very well informed from London.</p>
                <p>Sent Department as 1004, repeated London and Moscow.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d53-f1" n="3" xml:id="d53-f1-content">
                <p>Gen. Georges Catroux, French Ambassador in the Soviet
                    Union.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d53-f2" n="4" xml:id="d53-f2-content">
                <p>For text of formula, see telegram 1970, February 26, 3 p.m., from London, <ref
                        target="#pg95">p. 95</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d53-f3" n="5" xml:id="d53-f3-content">
                <p>
                    Alexander Efremovich Bogomolov, Soviet Ambassador in
                    France.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
